export const E1={title:'The respiratory therapist, the event and professional foundations',hook:'During the COVID-19 pandemic, respiratory therapists ran ventilators, managed oxygen and stood beside patients in ICUs around the clock. They care for premature babies, people with asthma and COPD, trauma patients and those at home on oxygen. Foundations of respiratory care is 20% of the test—the largest share, tied with three others.',objectives:['Describe the Respiratory Therapy event, its test plan and its seven skills.','Explain the history, education, credentials and practice settings of respiratory therapists.','Apply ethical, legal and communication principles in respiratory care.','Use evidence-based practice terms such as validity, reliability, sensitivity and specificity.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Respiratory Therapy has two rounds. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes**: foundations of respiratory care **20%**, applied anatomy and physiology **10%**, assessment of respiratory disorders **20%**, cardiopulmonary diseases **20%**, basic therapeutics **20%** and patient education and long-term care **10%**. References are *Egan’s Fundamentals of Respiratory Care*, *DHO: Health Science*, the American Association for Respiratory Care (AARC) and the “Early Connection: Defining Respiratory Therapy Pathways” webinar.',
'Top scorers advance to **Round Two**: **I** basic airway management (4 minutes), **II** donning and removing isolation garments (4), **III** oxygen by nasal cannula without humidification (6), **IV** pulse oximetry (4), **V** assessment of the newborn (5), **VI** measuring and recording vital signs (8) and **VII** hand-held nebulizer (8). Bring photo ID, a **manual watch with a second hand**, two pairs of non-latex gloves, a gown, goggles or safety glasses, a mask and an eye shield. You need **70%** on the combined skills; ties go to the higher test score.',
{t:'table',caption:'Written test plan',head:['Area','Weight'],rows:[['Foundations of respiratory care','20%'],['Assessment of respiratory disorders','20%'],['Cardiopulmonary diseases','20%'],['Basic therapeutics','20%'],['Applied anatomy and physiology','10%'],['Patient education and long-term care','10%']]}]],
['History and credentials',[
'Respiratory care grew from **oxygen technicians** in the 1940s who managed oxygen tents and cylinders. The **AARC** traces its roots to the Inhalation Therapy Association founded in **1947**. Polio epidemics and the invention of mechanical ventilators expanded the field. Public-health history matters too: the CDC first described the illness later named **AIDS** in **1981**, which transformed infection-control practice and the use of standard precautions.',
'Today respiratory therapists complete at least an **associate degree** (many earn bachelor’s or master’s degrees) from a **CoARC**-accredited program. The **NBRC** awards the entry-level **Certified Respiratory Therapist (CRT)** and advanced **Registered Respiratory Therapist (RRT)** credentials, plus specialties such as neonatal/pediatric (**RRT-NPS**), adult critical care (**RRT-ACCS**), pulmonary function (**RPFT**) and sleep disorders. Nearly every state requires a license. Job growth is faster than average because of an aging population and chronic lung disease.',
order('Path to practice',['High school with science and math','Accredited respiratory care program (associate degree or higher)','Clinical rotations in adult, pediatric and neonatal care','Pass NBRC exams for CRT, then RRT','State license','Specialty credentials and continuing education'])]],
['Where RTs work and what they do',[
'Respiratory therapists work in **intensive care units**, **emergency departments**, **neonatal and pediatric ICUs**, general hospital floors, **pulmonary function labs**, **sleep labs**, **pulmonary rehabilitation**, long-term acute care, skilled nursing facilities, **home care** and transport teams. They assess patients, give **oxygen** and **aerosol medications**, perform **airway management** and **airway clearance**, manage **mechanical ventilators**, draw and analyze **arterial blood gases**, respond to codes and rapid responses and educate patients and families.',
'RTs follow physician orders and **protocols**—approved plans that let therapists assess and adjust therapy within set limits. They work closely with physicians, nurses, pharmacists, physical therapists and dietitians. Clear communication with the team, accurate documentation and timely reporting of changes are part of every task.',
"RTs are core members of **rapid response** and **code** teams: they manage the airway, ventilate with a bag-valve-mask, assist with intubation, confirm tube placement with capnography and set up the ventilator. In transport, they keep critically ill newborns and adults breathing safely between hospitals by ground or air.",
match('Setting and task',[['ICU','Managing a mechanical ventilator'],['Emergency department','Airway management during a code'],['NICU','Supporting a premature infant’s breathing'],['Pulmonary function lab','Spirometry testing'],['Home care','Setting up home oxygen'],['Pulmonary rehab','Exercise and breathing education for COPD']])]],
['Ethics, law and communication',[
'RTs follow the **AARC Statement of Ethics and Professional Conduct**: respect patient autonomy and dignity, keep information confidential under **HIPAA**, practice within their **scope** and license, report errors honestly and promote safe, evidence-based care. **Informed consent** applies to procedures, and patients may refuse therapy. **Advance directives** (living wills, do-not-resuscitate orders) guide care at the end of life, and RTs often participate in withdrawing ventilator support.',
'Every rating sheet rewards **appropriate verbal and nonverbal communication**. Introduce yourself, identify the patient with **two identifiers**, explain each procedure in plain words, obtain consent, watch the patient’s reaction and **document** in the treatment notes. Patients who are short of breath may not be able to talk much; ask yes-or-no questions and give them time.',
sort('Ethical or unethical?',['Ethical practice','Unethical practice'],[['Identifying the patient with two identifiers',0],['Documenting a treatment you did not give',1],['Respecting a patient’s refusal of a nebulizer',0],['Sharing a patient’s diagnosis on social media',1],['Reporting a medication error',0],['Performing a procedure outside your license',1]])]],
['Evidence-based practice',[
'Respiratory care decisions should rest on good evidence. A test is **valid** if it **accurately measures what it is intended to measure**; it is **reliable** if it gives **consistent** results when repeated. **Sensitivity** is the ability to detect people who have a condition, and **specificity** is the ability to correctly identify people who do not. A pulse oximeter, for example, must be both accurate (valid) and consistent (reliable).',
'The strongest evidence usually comes from **systematic reviews** and **randomized controlled trials**, followed by cohort and case-control studies, case reports and expert opinion. Clinical practice guidelines—such as those from the AARC, the American Thoracic Society and the Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD)—summarize evidence into recommendations.',
"Asking a focused clinical question in **PICO** form—**P**atient or problem, **I**ntervention, **C**omparison, **O**utcome—helps find the right evidence, for example: “In adults with COPD exacerbations (P), does BiPAP (I) compared with oxygen alone (C) reduce intubation (O)?”",
match('Evidence terms',[['Validity','Measures what it is intended to measure'],['Reliability','Gives consistent results'],['Sensitivity','Detects those who have the condition'],['Specificity','Identifies those who do not have it'],['Randomized controlled trial','Participants randomly assigned to treatments'],['Protocol','Approved plan RTs follow to adjust therapy']])]],
['Practice: professional judgment',[
kase('Protocol and communication',{a:{text:'A protocol allows you to adjust oxygen to keep a patient’s SpO₂ at 92–96%. The patient’s SpO₂ is 99% on 4 L/min by nasal cannula. What do you do? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Lower the flow within the protocol, recheck SpO₂ and document','b','Correct. Protocols let RTs titrate oxygen safely.',true],['Leave it—higher is always better','b','Excess oxygen has risks; protocols set targets.',false]]},b:{text:'The patient asks why you changed the oxygen. What do you say?',choices:[['Explain in plain words that her oxygen level is healthy and you will keep checking it','c','Correct. Clear communication builds trust.',true],['“It’s protocol—don’t worry about it.”','c','Explain in a way the patient understands.',false]]},c:{text:'You recheck in 15 minutes, document the change and report it to the nurse.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Round One: 50 questions in 60 minutes with four 20% areas; Round Two draws on seven skills and requires 70%; bring a manual watch with a second hand.','RTs earn at least an associate degree, pass NBRC exams (CRT, RRT, specialties) and are licensed; they work in ICUs, NICUs, EDs, labs, rehab and homes; the AARC began in 1947 and the CDC recognized AIDS in 1981.','Ethics: autonomy, confidentiality, scope and honesty; validity = measures what it should, reliability = consistent; evidence-based protocols guide therapy.'],terms:[['RRT','Registered Respiratory Therapist, the advanced NBRC credential.'],['Validity','How accurately a test measures what it is intended to measure.'],['Protocol','An approved plan allowing RTs to assess and adjust therapy.']],mcqs:[q('In what year did the CDC recognize AIDS?','1981','1975','1977','1979','HOSA sample question.'),q('What is the term for a test that accurately measures what it is intended to measure?','Validity','Sensitivity','Specificity','Reliability','HOSA sample question.'),q('Which organization awards the CRT and RRT credentials?','NBRC','AARC','CoARC','CDC','The National Board for Respiratory Care.'),q('What minimum degree do respiratory therapists earn?','Associate degree','High school diploma','Doctorate','No degree','From an accredited program.'),q('Reliability means a test:','Gives consistent results','Is always correct','Is inexpensive','Is fast','Repeatability.'),q('Which item must RT competitors bring for vital signs?','A manual watch with a second hand','A smartwatch','A phone','A thermometer','No iWatches.'),q('How many identifiers are used in RT skills?','Two','One','Three','None','Per the rating sheets.'),q('Which percentage of the test is cardiopulmonary diseases?','20%','10%','4%','46%','One of four 20% areas.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the education and credentials of respiratory therapists, three settings where they work and how evidence-based practice and ethics guide their care.',rubric:[['Education and credentials',['associate','accredited','coarc','nbrc','crt','rrt','license']],['Settings and tasks',['icu','nicu','emergency','home','pulmonary function','ventilator','oxygen','aerosol']],['Evidence-based practice',['validity','reliability','guidelines','randomized','protocol']],['Ethics',['autonomy','confidential','hipaa','scope','consent','document']]],model:'Respiratory therapists complete at least an associate degree from a CoARC-accredited program that includes adult, pediatric and neonatal clinical rotations. They take NBRC exams to earn the Certified Respiratory Therapist credential and then the Registered Respiratory Therapist credential, may add specialty credentials such as RRT-NPS for neonatal and pediatric care, and must hold a state license. In the ICU they manage mechanical ventilators and draw arterial blood gases; in the NICU they support premature infants’ breathing; and in home care they set up oxygen and teach patients with COPD; they also work in emergency departments, pulmonary function and sleep labs and rehabilitation. Evidence-based practice means choosing therapies supported by research such as randomized controlled trials and guidelines, using valid and reliable tests and following approved protocols that let RTs adjust oxygen or aerosol therapy within set limits. Ethically, RTs respect patient autonomy and the right to refuse, obtain consent, keep information confidential under HIPAA, practice within their scope, report errors honestly and document every treatment accurately.'},sources:[RULES,AARC,NBRC,BLS_RT,CDC_AIDS]};

export const E2={title:'Safety, infection control and isolation garments (Skill II)',hook:'Oxygen does not burn—but it makes everything else burn faster and hotter. Respiratory therapists handle high-pressure gas cylinders, electrical equipment and patients with contagious lung infections every day. Skill II tests whether you can put on and take off isolation garments without contaminating yourself.',objectives:['Explain standard and transmission-based precautions for respiratory infections.','Don and remove isolation garments following HOSA Skill II.','Handle medical gas cylinders and oxygen safely.','Apply equipment cleaning and electrical safety principles.'],sections:[
['Infection control for respiratory care',[
'**Standard precautions** apply to every patient: hand hygiene, PPE based on expected exposure, respiratory hygiene and cough etiquette, safe injection practices and cleaning equipment. **Transmission-based precautions** add protection: **contact** (gown and gloves; MRSA, *C. difficile*, RSV in some settings), **droplet** (surgical mask within close range; influenza, pertussis, many respiratory viruses) and **airborne** (fit-tested **N95 respirator** and an airborne infection isolation room with negative pressure; tuberculosis, measles, chickenpox).',
'Respiratory procedures such as nebulizer treatments, suctioning, intubation and CPAP can be **aerosol-generating**, increasing the risk of spreading infection, so RTs often wear **eye protection** and respirators during them. Reusable equipment is cleaned and disinfected or sterilized according to its risk level, and disposable equipment is used for one patient only.',
"**Hand hygiene** remains the single most important infection-control step: use alcohol-based hand rub before and after patient contact, before clean or aseptic tasks and after touching the patient’s surroundings, and wash with soap and water when hands are visibly soiled or after caring for patients with *C. difficile*. RTs also receive the **hepatitis B** and annual **influenza** vaccines and are **fit-tested** for N95 respirators.",
sort('Which precautions?',['Contact','Droplet','Airborne'],[['Tuberculosis',2],['Influenza',1],['MRSA wound',0],['Measles',2],['Pertussis',1],['C. difficile',0]])]],
['Donning garments: Skill II steps 1–6',[
'Skill II has 10 steps in 4 minutes. **1.** Assemble equipment. **2.** Remove jewelry and pull up long sleeves. **3.** Hand hygiene. **4. Gown:** lift it by placing your **hands inside the shoulders**; work your arms into the sleeves with a gentle twist (roll the cuffs if too long); place your hands inside the **neckband**, adjust and **tie at the back of the neck**; **secure the waist ties**.',
'**5. Mask:** secure it **under the chin** covering mouth and nose; place elastic bands behind the ears or tie the **top ties first, then the bottom**; **pinch the nose piece** to secure it. **6. Gloves:** put them on so they **cover the top cuff of the gown**. The judge then says, “Skill completed.”',
order('Donning order',['Remove jewelry; pull up sleeves; hand hygiene','Gown: hands inside shoulders, arms in','Tie the neck, then the waist','Mask under the chin; top ties, then bottom','Pinch the nose piece','Gloves over the gown cuffs'])]],
['Removing garments: Skill II steps 7–10',[
'The HOSA RT sheet removes PPE in this order: **7.** **Untie the waist ties** and loosen the gown at the waist (while still gloved). **8. Gloves:** grasp the **outside of the cuff** of one glove and pull it off **inside out**; place your **bare hand inside the cuff** of the other glove and pull it off inside out; discard in the **infectious waste** container; perform hand hygiene.',
'**9. Gown:** untie the **neck ties** and loosen the gown at the shoulders, handling **only the inside**; fold the gown in half, **roll** it and discard it in infectious waste. **10. Mask:** untie the **bottom ties first**, then the top (or remove from behind the ears), hold it **by the top ties only** and drop it in infectious waste; perform hand hygiene.',
order('Removal order (RT sheet)',['Untie the gown waist ties','Remove gloves inside out; hand hygiene','Untie neck ties; touch only the inside of the gown','Fold, roll and discard the gown','Mask: bottom ties, then top; hold by the top ties','Hand hygiene']),
flip('Skill II details',[['Gown lift','Hands inside the shoulders'],['Mask on','Top ties first; pinch the nose'],['Gloves','Cover the top cuff of the gown'],['First removal step','Untie the gown waist'],['Mask off','Bottom ties first; hold by the top ties'],['Disposal','Infectious waste container']])]],
['Medical gas and oxygen safety',[
'Oxygen **supports combustion**: in an oxygen-rich environment, materials ignite more easily and burn hotter. Post **“No smoking—oxygen in use”** signs, keep oxygen at least several feet from flames, candles, gas stoves and sparks, avoid petroleum-based products (use water-based lip balm), and never let patients smoke while using oxygen—home oxygen fires cause serious burns every year. In Skill III you verbalize checking that there are **no sparks or flames** nearby.',
"Liquid oxygen systems used at home are extremely cold and can cause frostbite if touched, so patients learn to fill portable units carefully. Oxygen **concentrators** pull oxygen from room air and need electricity, so patients keep a backup cylinder for power outages.",
'In the United States, medical **oxygen cylinders are green** (international standard: white). Cylinders hold gas at high pressure—a full **E cylinder** (common portable size) is about **2,200 psi**. Store cylinders upright and **secured** in racks or carts, never drop or drag them, keep them away from heat, and use the correct **regulator** (pin-index safety system on small cylinders prevents connecting the wrong gas). **Crack** the valve briefly before attaching a regulator to clear dust, and stand to the side.',
sort('Safe or unsafe with oxygen?',['Safe','Unsafe'],[['Water-based lip balm',0],['Smoking while on a nasal cannula',1],['Cylinder secured upright in a cart',0],['Petroleum jelly on the nose',1],['“No smoking” sign on the door',0],['Storing a cylinder next to a space heater',1]])]],
['Equipment cleaning and electrical safety',[
'Respiratory equipment is classified by risk: **critical** items (entering sterile tissue) are sterilized; **semicritical** items that touch mucous membranes (laryngoscope blades, some nebulizer parts, ventilator circuits for reuse) need **high-level disinfection**; **noncritical** items (stethoscopes, pulse oximeter probes, blood pressure cuffs) are cleaned with low-level disinfectant between patients. Hand-held nebulizers are rinsed with **sterile water** and air-dried after each use, or discarded if soiled.',
'Electrical safety: use **three-prong grounded plugs**, inspect cords for fraying, keep liquids away from equipment, and report equipment that sparks, shocks or overheats. **Alarms** on monitors and ventilators must never be silenced and ignored—always check the patient first.',
"Other workplace hazards include **needlesticks** during blood gas sampling (activate safety devices and use sharps containers), **back injuries** from moving patients and heavy cylinders (use carts and proper body mechanics) and **latex allergy** (use non-latex supplies). Report injuries and near misses so systems can improve.",
match('Cleaning level',[['Laryngoscope blade','High-level disinfection'],['Pulse oximeter probe','Low-level disinfection between patients'],['Surgical instrument','Sterilization'],['Hand-held nebulizer after use','Rinse with sterile water and air-dry'],['Frayed power cord','Remove from service and report'],['Ventilator alarm','Check the patient first']])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Isolation room',{a:{text:'A patient with suspected tuberculosis needs a nebulizer treatment. What PPE do you need?',choices:[['A fit-tested N95 respirator, plus gown, gloves and eye protection for the aerosol procedure','b','Correct. TB is airborne, and nebulizers generate aerosols.',true],['A surgical mask only','b','A surgical mask does not protect against airborne TB.',false]]},b:{text:'You finish and begin removing PPE. Following the HOSA RT sheet, what comes first?',choices:[['Untie the gown waist ties, then remove gloves','c','Correct. The RT sheet loosens the waist first.',true],['Remove the respirator first','c','The mask comes off last.',false]]},c:{text:'You remove each item into the infectious waste container and perform hand hygiene. Safe exit.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Standard precautions for all; contact (gown, gloves), droplet (mask) and airborne (N95, negative-pressure room) precautions; aerosol-generating procedures need extra protection.','Skill II: gown by the inside shoulders (neck then waist), mask under the chin with top ties first and nose pinched, gloves over cuffs; remove by untying the waist, gloves, gown (inside only, fold and roll), mask (bottom ties first), hand hygiene.','Oxygen supports combustion—no smoking, flames or petroleum products; U.S. oxygen cylinders are green, stored upright and secured; clean equipment by risk level.'],terms:[['Aerosol-generating procedure','A procedure that can spread infectious particles in the air.'],['N95 respirator','A fitted mask that filters at least 95% of airborne particles.'],['Pin-index safety system','Pins on small cylinders that prevent connecting the wrong gas.']],mcqs:[q('Which precautions are used for tuberculosis?','Airborne','Contact','Droplet','Standard only','N95 and negative pressure.'),q('In Skill II, how is the gown lifted?','By placing hands inside the shoulders','By the waist ties','By the outside front','By the sleeves','Touch the inside.'),q('How are mask ties secured?','Top ties first, then bottom','Bottom first','Only the top','They are not tied','Then pinch the nose.'),q('Per the RT sheet, what is the first removal step?','Untie the waist ties','Remove the mask','Remove the gown neck ties','Wash hands','Loosen the gown at the waist.'),q('What color are medical oxygen cylinders in the United States?','Green','Blue','Red','Yellow','International standard is white.'),q('Why is oxygen a fire hazard?','It supports combustion','It is explosive by itself','It is flammable gas','It is not a hazard','Things burn faster.'),q('How are hand-held nebulizers cleaned after use?','Rinsed with sterile water and air-dried','Wiped with a dry towel','Boiled in tap water','Never cleaned','Or discarded if soiled.'),q('What should you do first when a ventilator alarms?','Check the patient','Silence it','Call maintenance','Unplug the ventilator','Patient first.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to don and remove isolation garments following HOSA Skill II, and describe three oxygen safety precautions you would teach a patient going home on oxygen.',rubric:[['Donning',['jewelry','hand hygiene','inside the shoulders','neck','waist','mask','top ties','pinch','gloves','cuff']],['Removal',['waist ties','gloves','inside out','hand hygiene','neck ties','inside','fold','roll','bottom ties','top ties']],['Disposal',['infectious waste','hand hygiene']],['Oxygen safety',['no smoking','flame','sign','petroleum','water-based','secure','upright','heat']]],model:'I assemble equipment, remove jewelry, pull up long sleeves and perform hand hygiene. I lift the gown by placing my hands inside the shoulders, work my arms into the sleeves, tie the neckband at the back of my neck and secure the waist ties. I place the mask under my chin over my mouth and nose, tie the top ties first and then the bottom ties, and pinch the nose piece. I put on gloves so they cover the top cuffs of the gown. To remove, I first untie the waist ties and loosen the gown. Then I remove the first glove by grasping the outside of the cuff and pulling it off inside out, slip my bare hand inside the cuff of the second glove and pull it off inside out, discard both in the infectious waste container and perform hand hygiene. I untie the neck ties, touching only the inside of the gown, fold it in half, roll it and discard it. I untie the mask’s bottom ties first, then the top ties, hold it by the top ties only, drop it in infectious waste and perform hand hygiene. For home oxygen I would teach the patient never to smoke or allow smoking nearby and to post “no smoking” signs; to keep oxygen away from open flames, gas stoves, candles and heaters; to use water-based rather than petroleum-based products on the lips and nose; and to store cylinders upright and secured away from heat.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_ISO,CDC_HH,NFPA_O2]};

export const E3={title:'Applied anatomy and physiology of breathing',hook:'Each day you breathe about 20,000 times, moving thousands of liters of air without thinking about it. A respiratory therapist needs to know exactly how that works—how the diaphragm creates pressure changes, how oxygen crosses into the blood and how the brain decides when to take the next breath—because every disease and treatment changes one of those steps.',objectives:['Identify structures of the upper and lower airways.','Explain the mechanics of ventilation using pressure and Boyle’s law.','Define lung volumes and capacities.','Describe gas exchange, oxygen transport and the control of breathing.'],sections:[
['The airways',[
'The **upper airway**—nose, pharynx and larynx—**warms, humidifies and filters** inspired air. The **larynx** contains the vocal cords and is protected by the **epiglottis**, which covers it during swallowing. The **lower airway** begins at the **trachea**, which divides at the **carina** into the right and left **mainstem bronchi**. The **right mainstem bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical**, so aspirated objects and endotracheal tubes placed too deep usually go to the right.',
'The bronchi branch into smaller **bronchioles** and finally into **alveoli**, where gas exchange happens. Adults have about **300–500 million alveoli** with a total surface area roughly the size of a tennis court. **Type I** alveolar cells form the thin exchange surface, and **type II** cells make **surfactant**, which lowers surface tension and keeps alveoli from collapsing. **Cilia** and mucus form the **mucociliary escalator** that clears particles; smoking damages it.',
label('resp','The respiratory system',[['nasal','Nasal cavity','Warms, humidifies and filters air.'],['pharynx','Pharynx','Shared passage for air and food.'],['larynx','Larynx','Voice box; protected by the epiglottis.'],['trachea','Trachea','Divides at the carina.'],['bronchi','Bronchi','Right mainstem is shorter, wider and more vertical.'],['bronchioles','Bronchioles','Small airways with smooth muscle.'],['alveoli','Alveoli','Gas exchange; type II cells make surfactant.'],['diaphragm','Diaphragm','Main muscle of inspiration.']])]],
['Mechanics of ventilation',[
'Air moves from **higher to lower pressure**. During **inspiration**, the **diaphragm contracts and flattens** and the **external intercostals** lift the ribs, increasing chest volume. By **Boyle’s law**, when volume increases, pressure decreases, so pressure in the lungs drops below atmospheric pressure and air flows in. **Expiration** at rest is **passive**: the muscles relax, the lungs recoil, pressure rises and air flows out. During exercise or respiratory distress, **accessory muscles** (sternocleidomastoid, scalenes) help inspiration and abdominal muscles help expiration.',
'**Compliance** describes how easily the lungs stretch—**low** in pulmonary fibrosis and ARDS (stiff lungs) and **high** in emphysema (floppy lungs that do not recoil). **Airway resistance** increases when airways narrow, as in asthma and bronchitis. The work of breathing rises with either problem.',
order('Steps of inspiration',['Diaphragm contracts and moves down','External intercostals lift the ribs','Chest volume increases','Lung pressure falls below atmospheric pressure','Air flows into the lungs']),
match('Mechanics terms',[['Boyle’s law','Pressure and volume change in opposite directions'],['Compliance','How easily the lungs stretch'],['Low compliance','Stiff lungs, as in fibrosis or ARDS'],['High compliance','Floppy lungs, as in emphysema'],['Airway resistance','Increased when airways narrow'],['Accessory muscles','Help breathing during distress']])]],
['Lung volumes and capacities',[
'**Tidal volume (VT)** is the air moved in a normal breath—about **500 mL** in an average adult (about 6–8 mL/kg of ideal body weight). **Inspiratory reserve volume** (about 3,000 mL) is the extra air you can inhale after a normal breath; **expiratory reserve volume** (about 1,100–1,200 mL) is the extra air you can exhale; **residual volume** (about 1,200 mL) stays in the lungs after maximal exhalation and cannot be measured by simple spirometry.',
'Capacities combine volumes: **vital capacity** (VT + IRV + ERV, about 4,500–4,800 mL) is the most air you can exhale after a maximal breath; **functional residual capacity** (ERV + RV, about 2,400 mL) remains after a normal exhalation; **total lung capacity** (all volumes, about 6,000 mL). **Obstructive** diseases trap air and raise RV and FRC; **restrictive** diseases reduce all volumes. **Minute ventilation** = tidal volume × respiratory rate (500 mL × 12 = 6 L/min).',
{t:'table',caption:'Approximate adult lung volumes',head:['Volume or capacity','Approximate value'],rows:[['Tidal volume','500 mL'],['Inspiratory reserve volume','3,000 mL'],['Expiratory reserve volume','1,200 mL'],['Residual volume','1,200 mL'],['Vital capacity','4,800 mL'],['Functional residual capacity','2,400 mL'],['Total lung capacity','6,000 mL']]},
chk('A patient breathes 16 times a minute with a tidal volume of 450 mL. What is the minute ventilation?',['7.2 L/min','4.5 L/min','16 L/min','0.45 L/min'],'450 mL × 16 = 7,200 mL = 7.2 L/min.')]],
['Gas exchange and transport',[
'Gas moves by **diffusion** from higher to lower **partial pressure**. Alveolar oxygen (about 100 mm Hg) diffuses into blood returning with lower oxygen (about 40 mm Hg), and carbon dioxide diffuses from the blood (about 46 mm Hg) into the alveoli (about 40 mm Hg). Diffusion is slowed when the membrane thickens (fibrosis, pulmonary edema) or surface area is lost (emphysema). Matching **ventilation and perfusion (V/Q)** is essential: a **shunt** is perfusion without ventilation (collapsed or fluid-filled alveoli), and **dead space** is ventilation without perfusion (as in pulmonary embolism).',
'About **98%** of oxygen travels bound to **hemoglobin**; the rest dissolves in plasma (measured as PaO₂). The **oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve** is S-shaped: hemoglobin stays highly saturated until PaO₂ falls below about 60 mm Hg, then saturation drops steeply. A **right shift** (acidosis, high CO₂, fever, high 2,3-DPG) releases oxygen to tissues more easily. **Carbon dioxide** travels mostly as **bicarbonate**, linking breathing to acid–base balance. **Carbon monoxide** binds hemoglobin far more tightly than oxygen.',
match('Gas exchange terms',[['Diffusion','Movement from high to low partial pressure'],['Shunt','Perfusion without ventilation'],['Dead space','Ventilation without perfusion'],['Hemoglobin','Carries about 98% of oxygen'],['Right shift','Hemoglobin releases oxygen more easily'],['Bicarbonate','Main form of CO₂ transport']])]],
['Control of breathing',[
'The **medulla oblongata** and **pons** in the brainstem set the rhythm of breathing. **Central chemoreceptors** in the medulla respond mainly to rising **carbon dioxide** (through changes in cerebrospinal fluid pH)—the main drive to breathe in healthy people. **Peripheral chemoreceptors** in the **carotid and aortic bodies** respond to low **oxygen** (especially when PaO₂ falls below about 60 mm Hg), high CO₂ and acidosis.',
'Some patients with severe chronic CO₂ retention may rely more on low-oxygen drive, and excessive oxygen can worsen their CO₂ levels—mainly by worsening V/Q matching—so oxygen is titrated to target saturations (often **88–92%** for these patients) rather than withheld. **Stretch receptors** in the lungs and **irritant receptors** in the airways also influence breathing and trigger coughing.',
"Breathing also helps control **acid–base balance**: breathing faster blows off CO₂ and raises pH, while breathing slower retains CO₂ and lowers pH.",
sort('Central or peripheral chemoreceptor?',['Central (medulla)','Peripheral (carotid and aortic bodies)'],[['Main response to rising CO₂ in healthy people',0],['Strong response to PaO₂ below 60 mm Hg',1],['Located in the carotid bodies',1],['Responds to cerebrospinal fluid pH',0],['Located near the aortic arch',1]])]],
['Practice: physiology in action',[
kase('Physiology reasoning',{a:{text:'A choking child aspirates a small toy. Which lung is it most likely to enter?',choices:[['The right lung, because the right mainstem bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical','b','Correct. Anatomy explains the pattern.',true],['The left lung, because it is larger','b','The left lung is smaller, and its bronchus angles more sharply.',false]]},b:{text:'A premature infant lacks surfactant. What happens to the alveoli?',choices:[['They collapse because surface tension is high','c','Correct. Surfactant lowers surface tension.',true],['They become larger','c','Without surfactant, alveoli collapse.',false]]},c:{text:'You connect anatomy and physiology to disease—exactly what the assessment and disease sections test.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Upper airway warms, humidifies and filters; the trachea divides at the carina; the right mainstem is wider, shorter and more vertical; type II cells make surfactant.','Inspiration is active (diaphragm contracts, volume rises, pressure falls—Boyle’s law); expiration is usually passive; compliance and resistance determine work of breathing.','Tidal volume ~500 mL, vital capacity ~4,800 mL, TLC ~6,000 mL; diffusion depends on partial pressures; ~98% of O₂ rides on hemoglobin; CO₂ is the main breathing drive via central chemoreceptors.'],terms:[['Surfactant','A substance that lowers surface tension and keeps alveoli open.'],['Tidal volume','The volume of air moved in a normal breath.'],['Compliance','How easily the lungs and chest wall stretch.']],mcqs:[q('Where does gas exchange occur?','Alveoli','Trachea','Bronchi','Larynx','Thin membranes.'),q('Which bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical?','Right mainstem','Left mainstem','Both are equal','Neither','Aspiration goes right.'),q('What is the main muscle of inspiration?','Diaphragm','Sternocleidomastoid','Internal intercostals','Abdominal muscles','Contracts and flattens.'),q('Normal adult tidal volume is about:','500 mL','50 mL','5,000 mL','1,200 mL','6–8 mL/kg IBW.'),q('Which cells make surfactant?','Type II alveolar cells','Type I alveolar cells','Goblet cells','Red blood cells','Lowers surface tension.'),q('Emphysema causes lung compliance to:','Increase','Decrease','Stay the same','Disappear','Floppy lungs.'),q('Most oxygen in the blood travels:','Bound to hemoglobin','Dissolved in plasma','As bicarbonate','In platelets','About 98%.'),q('The main chemical drive to breathe in healthy people is:','Carbon dioxide','Oxygen','Nitrogen','Glucose','Central chemoreceptors.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how air moves into and out of the lungs and how oxygen reaches the tissues, then describe how emphysema and pulmonary fibrosis each disrupt these processes.',rubric:[['Ventilation mechanics',['diaphragm','intercostals','volume','pressure','boyle','passive','recoil']],['Gas exchange and transport',['alveoli','diffusion','partial pressure','hemoglobin','98%','dissociation']],['Emphysema',['emphysema','compliance','increase','recoil','air trapping','surface area']],['Fibrosis',['fibrosis','stiff','compliance','decrease','thick','diffusion','restrictive']]],model:'During inspiration the diaphragm contracts and flattens and the external intercostals lift the ribs, enlarging the chest. By Boyle’s law, the larger volume lowers pressure in the lungs below atmospheric pressure, so air flows in through the conducting airways to the alveoli. Expiration at rest is passive: the muscles relax, the elastic lungs recoil, pressure rises and air flows out. In the alveoli, oxygen diffuses across the thin membrane from a higher partial pressure in the alveolar air into the lower partial pressure of the pulmonary capillary blood, while carbon dioxide diffuses the other way. About 98% of the oxygen binds to hemoglobin, and the S-shaped oxyhemoglobin curve allows hemoglobin to load oxygen in the lungs and release it in the tissues, especially when acidity or temperature shifts the curve to the right. In emphysema, alveolar walls are destroyed, so compliance increases and elastic recoil is lost; air becomes trapped, residual volume rises, expiration takes more effort and the surface area for diffusion shrinks. In pulmonary fibrosis, scarring makes the lungs stiff, so compliance falls and all lung volumes decrease—a restrictive pattern—and the thickened membrane slows diffusion, causing low oxygen, especially with exercise.'},sources:[RULES,OSA_RESP,OSA_GAS,NHLBI_LUNGS]};

export const E4={title:'Physical assessment, breath sounds and vital signs (Skill VI)',hook:'Before any test results come back, a respiratory therapist can learn a great deal by watching how a patient breathes, listening with a stethoscope and counting a pulse. Accessory muscle use, wheezes, crackles, a respiratory rate of 32—each tells a story. Assessment of respiratory disorders is 20% of the test, and Skill VI is worth 60 points.',objectives:['Perform a respiratory history and inspection, including signs of respiratory distress.','Identify normal and adventitious breath sounds and their causes.','Measure pulse, respirations and blood pressure by the palpatory and auscultatory methods following HOSA Skill VI.','Recognize abnormal vital signs that need immediate reporting.'],sections:[
['History and inspection',[
'A respiratory history covers **dyspnea** (when, how severe, what triggers it), **cough** (dry or productive, duration), **sputum** (color, amount, blood—**hemoptysis**), **wheezing**, **chest pain** (pleuritic pain worsens with breathing), smoking history in **pack-years** (packs per day × years smoked), occupational exposures (asbestos, silica, coal dust) and home oxygen or nebulizer use. Scales such as the **modified Medical Research Council (mMRC)** dyspnea scale grade breathlessness.',
'**Inspection** looks for **respiratory rate and pattern**, **accessory muscle use**, **nasal flaring**, **retractions** (in children), **pursed-lip breathing**, **tripod positioning**, the ability to speak in full sentences, **cyanosis** (bluish lips or nail beds, a late sign of low oxygen), **digital clubbing** (chronic lung disease, cystic fibrosis, lung cancer), **barrel chest** (increased front-to-back diameter in COPD), chest symmetry and **edema**. Patterns include **Kussmaul** (deep and rapid—metabolic acidosis), **Cheyne-Stokes** (cycles of deep breathing and apnea—heart failure, brain injury) and **apnea**.',
match('Inspection findings',[['Tripod position','Leaning forward on the arms to breathe'],['Digital clubbing','Chronic lung disease or lung cancer'],['Barrel chest','Air trapping in COPD'],['Kussmaul breathing','Deep, rapid breaths in metabolic acidosis'],['Cheyne-Stokes','Cycles of deep breathing and apnea'],['Pack-years','Packs per day × years smoked']])]],
['Palpation, percussion and auscultation',[
'**Palpation** assesses chest expansion symmetry and **tactile fremitus** (vibration when the patient speaks—**increased** over consolidation such as pneumonia, **decreased** with pleural effusion, pneumothorax or air trapping). **Percussion** produces **resonance** over normal lung, **dullness** over consolidation or fluid and **hyperresonance** over air trapping or pneumothorax. Tracheal deviation away from a tension pneumothorax is an emergency sign.',
'**Auscultation** with the stethoscope diaphragm compares both sides from top to bottom. **Normal** sounds are **vesicular** (soft, low-pitched, over most of the lungs), **bronchovesicular** and **bronchial** (loud, over the trachea). **Adventitious** sounds: **crackles** (discontinuous; fluid or reopening alveoli—pneumonia, heart failure, atelectasis, fibrosis), **wheezes** (continuous, musical; narrowed airways—asthma, COPD), **rhonchi** (low-pitched, snoring; secretions in large airways, may clear with coughing), **stridor** (high-pitched inspiratory sound from **upper airway obstruction**—an emergency) and **pleural friction rub** (grating, from inflamed pleura). **Diminished** sounds suggest poor air movement, effusion or pneumothorax.',
match('Breath sound → cause',[['Crackles','Fluid or reopening alveoli'],['Wheezes','Narrowed lower airways'],['Rhonchi','Secretions in large airways'],['Stridor','Upper airway obstruction'],['Pleural friction rub','Inflamed pleura'],['Absent breath sounds on one side','Pneumothorax or large effusion']]),
sort('Percussion note',['Dull','Hyperresonant','Resonant'],[['Pleural effusion',0],['Pneumothorax',1],['Normal lung',2],['Lobar pneumonia',0],['Severe emphysema',1]])]],
['Vital signs: Skill VI pulse and respirations',[
'Skill VI has 15 steps in **8 minutes**. **1.** Assemble equipment. **2.** Hand hygiene and gloves. **3.** Greet and introduce yourself. **4.** Identify the patient with **two identifiers**. **5. Radial pulse:** support the hand and arm comfortably with the **palm down**; locate the radial artery with your **middle and index fingers** on the thumb side of the inside wrist; apply light pressure and count for **30 seconds and multiply by 2** or count a **full minute**.',
'**6. Respirations:** keep your fingers in the pulse position so the patient is unaware, and count for 30 seconds × 2 or a full minute—**one inspiration plus one expiration equals one respiration**. **7.** Verbalize counts **within ±2** of the judge’s. **8.** Describe the **pulse quality** (volume or strength and rhythm) and **respiration quality** (depth and rhythm). **9.** Record on the graphic form. Usual adult ranges: pulse **60–100**, respirations **12–20**. Count irregular rhythms for a full minute.',
order('Pulse and respirations',['Support the arm, palm down','Two fingers on the thumb side of the wrist','Count the pulse (30 s × 2 or 60 s)','Keep fingers in place and watch the chest','Count respirations (30 s × 2 or 60 s)','Verbalize counts and describe quality','Record on the graphic form'])]],
['Vital signs: Skill VI blood pressure',[
'The RT sheet uses the **palpatory method first**. **10a.** Place the **center of the cuff over the brachial artery**. **b.** Find the **radial pulse** and keep your fingers on it. **c.** Close the valve clockwise. **d.** Inflate until the **radial pulse disappears**. **e.** Inflate **30 mm Hg above** that point. **f.** Release slowly while watching the gauge. **g.** When the pulse returns, note the reading—the **palpatory systolic pressure**.',
'**h.** Place the stethoscope earpieces in your ears. **i.** Palpate the brachial artery. **j.** Place the stethoscope over it. **k.** Inflate to **30 mm Hg above the palpatory systolic pressure**. **l.** Deflate at **2–3 mm Hg per second**. **m.** Note the **first sound (systolic)** and **last sound (diastolic)**. **n.** Deflate completely, remove the cuff and make the patient comfortable. **o–r.** Record accurately and replace equipment; readings must be **within ±2 mm Hg** of the judge’s. **11–15.** Hand hygiene; perform all vital signs smoothly and in logical order; observe checkpoints (call signal within reach, bed lowered, good body alignment, report changes to the nurse); communicate and use standard precautions. The palpatory step prevents missing an **auscultatory gap**.',
order('Palpatory then auscultatory BP',['Center the cuff over the brachial artery','Feel the radial pulse; close the valve','Inflate until the radial pulse disappears, then 30 mm Hg more','Deflate slowly; note when the pulse returns (palpatory systolic)','Stethoscope over the brachial artery','Inflate to 30 mm Hg above palpatory systolic','Deflate 2–3 mm Hg/s; note first and last sounds']),
chk('A patient’s radial pulse disappears at 128 mm Hg. To what level should you inflate before auscultating?',['158 mm Hg (30 above the palpatory systolic)','128 mm Hg','200 mm Hg always','98 mm Hg'],'Palpatory systolic + 30 mm Hg.')]],
['Recognizing abnormal findings',[
'Report promptly: **tachypnea** (respiratory rate above 20, and especially above 30), **bradypnea** (below 12) or pauses, **tachycardia** above 100 or **bradycardia** below 60 with symptoms, **hypotension** or a large blood pressure change, **SpO₂** below the ordered target, new **confusion**, **cyanosis**, **stridor**, inability to speak in full sentences and accessory muscle use. Rising respiratory rate is often the **earliest** sign that a patient is getting worse.',
'Fever increases oxygen demand and respiratory rate. Pain, anxiety and exertion raise heart and breathing rates; medications such as opioids slow breathing. Always compare readings with the patient’s baseline and trends, and repeat a measurement if it seems inconsistent with how the patient looks.',
"Temperature, pain and level of consciousness complete the picture; a confused, drowsy patient may be retaining CO₂.",
{t:'w',kind:'vitalsreport',title:'Report or record?',prompt:'Decide which adult readings fall within the usual teaching ranges and which you would report to the nurse.',person:'patient',reportTo:'nurse'}]],
['Practice: putting assessment together',[
kase('Assessment at the bedside',{a:{text:'A 60-year-old with COPD is sitting in the tripod position, using neck muscles to breathe and speaking in two-word phrases. RR 30. What does this tell you? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['He is in respiratory distress and needs prompt assessment and reporting','b','Correct. Tripod position, accessory muscles and short phrases signal distress.',true],['His breathing is normal for COPD','b','These are signs of distress.',false]]},b:{text:'You hear diffuse expiratory wheezes and diminished sounds at both bases. Which is the likely problem?',choices:[['Bronchospasm and air trapping from a COPD exacerbation','c','Correct. Wheezes mean narrowed airways.',true],['Upper airway obstruction','c','That would cause stridor.',false]]},c:{text:'You check SpO₂, notify the provider and prepare for a bronchodilator treatment per orders.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['History: dyspnea, cough, sputum, hemoptysis, pack-years and exposures; inspection: rate, pattern, accessory muscles, tripod, cyanosis, clubbing, barrel chest.','Fremitus increases with consolidation; percussion dull (fluid/consolidation) or hyperresonant (air); crackles (fluid), wheezes (narrowed airways), rhonchi (secretions), stridor (upper airway emergency).','Skill VI: two identifiers; pulse and respirations 30 s × 2 or 60 s, within ±2; palpatory systolic, inflate 30 above, deflate 2–3 mm Hg/s, within ±2 mm Hg; checkpoints and reporting.'],terms:[['Adventitious sounds','Abnormal breath sounds such as crackles or wheezes.'],['Tactile fremitus','Vibration felt on the chest when a patient speaks.'],['Palpatory systolic pressure','The pressure at which the radial pulse returns as the cuff deflates.']],mcqs:[q('High-pitched inspiratory sound from upper airway obstruction is:','Stridor','Wheeze','Crackle','Rhonchi','Emergency.'),q('Crackles suggest:','Fluid in or reopening of alveoli','Upper airway obstruction','Normal lungs','Pleural friction only','Pneumonia, heart failure, atelectasis.'),q('Percussion over a pneumothorax sounds:','Hyperresonant','Dull','Flat','Normal','Excess air.'),q('In Skill VI, how far above the palpatory systolic pressure do you inflate?','30 mm Hg','10 mm Hg','60 mm Hg','100 mm Hg','Then auscultate.'),q('How fast do you deflate the cuff in the RT sheet?','2–3 mm Hg per second','10 mm Hg per second','All at once','1 mm Hg per minute','Even rate.'),q('One respiration equals:','One inspiration plus one expiration','Only inspiration','Two breaths','One heartbeat','Count both together.'),q('Digital clubbing is associated with:','Chronic lung disease','Acute asthma only','Normal aging','Dehydration','Also cystic fibrosis and lung cancer.'),q('Which is often the earliest sign a patient is worsening?','Rising respiratory rate','Lower temperature','Better appetite','Slower pulse','Watch the respiratory rate.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would measure blood pressure following HOSA Skill VI, including the palpatory method, and explain why the palpatory step matters.',rubric:[['Setup',['two identifiers','hand hygiene','gloves','cuff','brachial artery','center']],['Palpatory method',['radial pulse','close the valve','disappears','30 mm hg','returns','palpatory systolic']],['Auscultatory method',['stethoscope','brachial','30 mm hg above','2-3 mm hg','first sound','systolic','last sound','diastolic']],['Accuracy and reasoning',['within 2','record','auscultatory gap','checkpoints','report']]],model:'After assembling equipment, performing hand hygiene, putting on gloves, greeting the patient and identifying them with two identifiers, I place the center of the cuff over the brachial artery. For the palpatory method, I find the radial pulse and keep my fingers on it, close the valve by turning it clockwise and inflate until the radial pulse disappears, then inflate 30 mm Hg more. I slowly release the pressure while watching the gauge, and the reading when the pulse returns is the palpatory systolic pressure. Next I position the stethoscope earpieces, palpate the brachial artery, place the stethoscope over it and inflate the cuff to 30 mm Hg above the palpatory systolic pressure. I deflate at an even 2–3 mm Hg per second, noting the first sound as the systolic pressure and the last sound as the diastolic pressure, then deflate completely, remove the cuff, make the patient comfortable and record the reading, which should be within ±2 mm Hg of the judge’s. The palpatory step matters because some patients have an auscultatory gap—a silent period between sounds—so inflating to a known level above the true systolic prevents me from underestimating the systolic pressure. I finish with hand hygiene, the checkpoints and reporting any changes to the nurse.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_BREATH,MEDLINE_VITALS]};

export const E5={title:'Pulse oximetry, arterial blood gases and diagnostic tests (Skill IV)',hook:'A clip on a fingertip shows 89%. A blood gas shows a pH of 7.28 and a PaCO₂ of 62. A chest X-ray shows a white-out of the left lower lobe. Respiratory therapists turn numbers and images into decisions—more oxygen, a bronchodilator, a call to the physician. Skill IV tests pulse oximetry, and blood gas interpretation appears throughout the test.',objectives:['Perform pulse oximetry following HOSA Skill IV and explain its limitations.','Interpret arterial blood gases for acid–base balance and oxygenation.','Describe spirometry patterns for obstructive and restrictive disease.','Recognize key findings on chest radiographs and other diagnostic tests.'],sections:[
['Pulse oximetry: Skill IV',[
'A **pulse oximeter** shines red and infrared light through tissue and estimates the percentage of hemoglobin carrying oxygen (**SpO₂**), along with the pulse rate. Skill IV has 18 steps in **4 minutes**. **1.** Obtain and review the order. **2.** Obtain equipment. **3.** Hand hygiene and gloves. **4.** Greet and identify the patient with **two identifiers**. **5.** Provide privacy. **6.** Explain and obtain consent. **7–8.** Assemble, **turn on and test** the equipment before applying it. **9.** Examine the fingers—**nail beds clear** and hands not visibly soiled.',
'**10.** Place the probe on the finger. **11.** Observe for an **adequate waveform** (a clear, regular pulse signal). **12.** Confirm the **SpO₂** and **heart rate** readings (the oximeter pulse should match a palpated pulse). **13.** Tell the patient and appropriate personnel the SpO₂ and verbalize whether it is in the **normal range**—typically **95–100%** on room air for healthy adults (targets such as 88–92% may be ordered for some patients with chronic CO₂ retention). **14–18.** Remove gloves, hand hygiene, document, communicate and use standard precautions.',
order('Skill IV sequence',['Review the order; obtain equipment','Hand hygiene and gloves','Greet; identify with two identifiers; privacy','Explain and obtain consent','Turn on and test the oximeter','Check that nail beds are clear and hands clean','Place the probe and watch for a good waveform','Confirm SpO₂ and heart rate; report whether normal','Remove gloves, hand hygiene, document'])]],
['Oximetry limitations',[
'Pulse oximeters can be **inaccurate** with **poor perfusion** (cold hands, shock, vasoconstriction), **motion**, **nail polish** or artificial nails, bright ambient light, **severe anemia** and abnormal hemoglobins. In **carbon monoxide poisoning**, standard oximeters can show a falsely **normal** SpO₂ because they cannot tell carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin; **CO-oximetry** on a blood sample is needed. The FDA has also warned that pulse oximeters may **overestimate** oxygen levels in people with **darker skin pigmentation**, so clinicians consider trends, symptoms and blood gases.',
'SpO₂ reflects **oxygenation only**—it does **not** measure ventilation or carbon dioxide. A patient on supplemental oxygen can have a normal SpO₂ while CO₂ rises dangerously. When ventilation is a concern, use **arterial blood gases** or **capnography** (end-tidal CO₂).',
sort('Could this affect SpO₂ accuracy?',['Yes—may cause error','No—usually fine'],[['Cold, poorly perfused fingers',0],['Dark nail polish',0],['Carbon monoxide poisoning',0],['Patient shivering and moving',0],['Warm hand with a strong pulse',1],['Clear nail bed and steady waveform',1]])]],
['Arterial blood gases',[
'An **ABG** measures **pH** (normal **7.35–7.45**), **PaCO₂** (35–45 mm Hg; the respiratory component), **HCO₃⁻** (22–26 mEq/L; the metabolic component), **PaO₂** (about **80–100 mm Hg** on room air) and **SaO₂**. Interpret in steps: (1) **pH**—below 7.35 is **acidemia**, above 7.45 **alkalemia**; (2) **PaCO₂**—if it moves opposite to pH (high CO₂ with low pH), the cause is **respiratory**; (3) **HCO₃⁻**—if it moves with pH (low HCO₃⁻ with low pH), the cause is **metabolic**; (4) **compensation**—the other system moves to bring pH back toward normal.',
'The memory aid **ROME**: **R**espiratory **O**pposite, **M**etabolic **E**qual. **Respiratory acidosis** comes from **hypoventilation** (COPD exacerbation, opioid overdose, neuromuscular weakness); **respiratory alkalosis** from **hyperventilation** (anxiety, pain, early asthma attack, pulmonary embolism); **metabolic acidosis** from DKA, lactic acidosis, kidney failure or diarrhea (the body compensates with deep, rapid breathing); **metabolic alkalosis** from vomiting or diuretics. Before an ABG from the radial artery, an **Allen test** checks collateral circulation from the ulnar artery.',
{t:'example',title:'Interpret an ABG',prompt:'pH 7.28, PaCO₂ 62 mm Hg, HCO₃⁻ 25 mEq/L, PaO₂ 58 mm Hg. (Values invented for practice.)',steps:['pH 7.28 is below 7.35: acidemia.','PaCO₂ 62 is high and moves opposite to pH: respiratory.','HCO₃⁻ 25 is normal: no metabolic compensation yet (acute).','PaO₂ 58 is low: hypoxemia.'],answer:'Acute (uncompensated) respiratory acidosis with hypoxemia—for example, from hypoventilation in a COPD exacerbation.'},
sort('Name the disorder',['Respiratory acidosis','Respiratory alkalosis','Metabolic acidosis','Metabolic alkalosis'],[['pH 7.30, PaCO₂ 58, HCO₃⁻ 24',0],['pH 7.50, PaCO₂ 28, HCO₃⁻ 24',1],['pH 7.25, PaCO₂ 30, HCO₃⁻ 14',2],['pH 7.52, PaCO₂ 46, HCO₃⁻ 34',3],['Opioid overdose with slow breathing',0],['Repeated vomiting',3]])]],
['Pulmonary function tests',[
'**Spirometry** measures how much and how fast air can be exhaled. The key values are the **forced vital capacity (FVC)**, the **forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV₁)** and their ratio. In **obstructive** diseases (asthma, COPD, bronchiectasis, cystic fibrosis), air cannot get out quickly: **FEV₁/FVC is below 0.70** (or below the lower limit of normal). A **significant bronchodilator response** (about a 12% and 200 mL increase in FEV₁ or FVC) supports **asthma**. In **restrictive** diseases (fibrosis, obesity, neuromuscular disease, chest wall deformities), volumes are small but the **ratio is normal or high**; **total lung capacity** measured by body plethysmography confirms restriction.',
'**Diffusion capacity (DLCO)** is reduced when the gas-exchange membrane is damaged (emphysema, fibrosis) but is often normal in asthma. **Peak expiratory flow** meters help patients with asthma monitor themselves at home using green, yellow and red zones. Patients must give maximal effort; poor effort makes results invalid.',
match('PFT pattern',[['FEV₁/FVC below 0.70','Obstruction'],['Normal ratio with low TLC','Restriction'],['FEV₁ improves 15% after albuterol','Asthma (reversible obstruction)'],['Low DLCO with obstruction','Emphysema'],['Low DLCO with restriction','Pulmonary fibrosis'],['Peak flow meter','Home asthma monitoring']])]],
['Chest imaging and other tests',[
'On a **chest X-ray**, air appears **black** and dense tissue and fluid appear **white**. **Consolidation** (pneumonia) appears as a white area, sometimes with **air bronchograms**; **pleural effusion** blunts the costophrenic angle; **pneumothorax** shows a visible pleural line with no lung markings beyond it; **hyperinflation** with a flattened diaphragm suggests COPD; **pulmonary edema** shows bilateral fluffy opacities and an enlarged heart in heart failure. RTs also confirm **endotracheal tube position** (tip about 3–5 cm above the carina).',
'**CT** gives detailed images (CT angiography for pulmonary embolism, high-resolution CT for fibrosis). **Sputum cultures** identify organisms; **capnography** monitors CO₂ continuously and confirms tube placement; **polysomnography** diagnoses sleep apnea; **bronchoscopy** looks inside the airways and takes samples; the **sweat chloride test** diagnoses cystic fibrosis.',
match('Chest X-ray finding',[['White area with air bronchograms','Pneumonia (consolidation)'],['Blunted costophrenic angle','Pleural effusion'],['Pleural line with no lung markings beyond it','Pneumothorax'],['Flattened diaphragm and hyperinflation','COPD'],['Bilateral fluffy opacities and large heart','Pulmonary edema'],['Tube tip 3–5 cm above the carina','Correct endotracheal tube position']])]],
['Practice: data interpretation',[
kase('Numbers that matter',{a:{text:'A patient rescued from a house fire has an SpO₂ of 99% but a headache and confusion. What should you suspect? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Carbon monoxide poisoning—SpO₂ can be falsely normal; obtain CO-oximetry','b','Correct. Standard oximeters cannot detect carboxyhemoglobin.',true],['Normal oxygenation—no action needed','b','The reading may be falsely reassuring.',false]]},b:{text:'What treatment should begin first for carbon monoxide poisoning?',choices:[['100% oxygen by nonrebreather mask','c','Correct. High-concentration oxygen speeds CO removal.',true],['Room air and observation','c','High-flow oxygen is needed.',false]]},c:{text:'You report the findings, start 100% oxygen per orders and document.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Skill IV: two identifiers, test the oximeter, clear nail beds, good waveform, confirm SpO₂ and heart rate, report whether normal (about 95–100%); oximetry measures oxygenation, not ventilation.','Oximetry errors: poor perfusion, motion, nail polish, carbon monoxide, darker skin may overestimate; ABG normals pH 7.35–7.45, PaCO₂ 35–45, HCO₃⁻ 22–26, PaO₂ 80–100; ROME.','Spirometry: FEV₁/FVC <0.70 = obstruction, normal ratio with low TLC = restriction; chest X-ray: consolidation, effusion, pneumothorax, hyperinflation, edema.'],terms:[['SpO₂','Oxygen saturation measured by pulse oximetry.'],['Respiratory acidosis','Low pH with high PaCO₂ from hypoventilation.'],['FEV₁/FVC','The ratio of air exhaled in one second to total forced exhalation.']],mcqs:[q('What is the normal arterial pH range?','7.35–7.45','7.0–7.2','7.5–7.7','6.8–7.0','Below 7.35 is acidemia.'),q('Normal PaCO₂ is:','35–45 mm Hg','80–100 mm Hg','22–26 mm Hg','10–20 mm Hg','The respiratory component.'),q('pH 7.30 with PaCO₂ 58 and normal HCO₃⁻ is:','Respiratory acidosis','Metabolic acidosis','Respiratory alkalosis','Metabolic alkalosis','Respiratory opposite.'),q('Which condition can give a falsely normal SpO₂?','Carbon monoxide poisoning','Fever','Dehydration','Asthma','Use CO-oximetry.'),q('In Skill IV, what should you check on the finger before placing the probe?','Nail beds are clear and hands not soiled','The fingerprint pattern','The ring size','Capillary refill only','Polish and dirt interfere.'),q('An FEV₁/FVC ratio below 0.70 indicates:','Obstruction','Restriction','Normal lungs','Pneumothorax','As in COPD.'),q('A blunted costophrenic angle on X-ray suggests:','Pleural effusion','Pneumothorax','Normal lungs','Emphysema','Fluid.'),q('What does pulse oximetry NOT measure?','Carbon dioxide level','Oxygen saturation','Pulse rate','Pulse waveform','Use ABG or capnography.')],frq:{prompt:'Interpret this blood gas and explain your steps: pH 7.48, PaCO₂ 30 mm Hg, HCO₃⁻ 23 mEq/L, PaO₂ 62 mm Hg in a patient with sudden shortness of breath after hip surgery. Then describe one cause consistent with these findings and how pulse oximetry would fit into her monitoring. (Values invented for practice.)',rubric:[['Step-by-step interpretation',['ph','7.48','alkalemia','paco2','30','low','respiratory']],['Classification',['respiratory alkalosis','acute','uncompensated','hco3','normal','hypoxemia','62']],['Cause',['pulmonary embolism','hyperventilation','dead space','surgery','clot']],['Oximetry role',['spo2','trend','oxygenation','not co2','waveform','report','limitations']]],model:'First, the pH of 7.48 is above 7.45, so she has alkalemia. Second, the PaCO₂ of 30 mm Hg is low and moves in the opposite direction from the pH, which points to a respiratory cause—she is hyperventilating and blowing off carbon dioxide. Third, the HCO₃⁻ of 23 mEq/L is normal, so the kidneys have not yet compensated, making this an acute, uncompensated respiratory alkalosis. Finally, the PaO₂ of 62 mm Hg is below the normal 80–100 range, so she also has hypoxemia. In a patient with sudden shortness of breath after hip surgery, a pulmonary embolism is a leading cause: the clot creates dead space and poor gas exchange, causing low oxygen, while breathlessness and anxiety drive hyperventilation and low CO₂. Pulse oximetry would let us continuously trend her oxygen saturation and titrate oxygen to the ordered target, but I would make sure the probe shows a good waveform and remember that SpO₂ measures only oxygenation, not CO₂, so repeat blood gases would be needed to assess ventilation. Any falling saturation would be reported immediately.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_PULSEOX,FDA_PULSEOX,MEDLINE_ABG,MEDLINE_PFT]};
